# bench/json-compare/main.ts run on sample.json: `<workload> <iterations> <at>:`
# and the checksum line it prints. Every comparator (simdjson, yyjson,
# RapidJSON, serde_json, Go) printed the same line for the same arguments when
# this was recorded, the f64 sum spelt each language's way; tests/run.js holds
# the Nish program to it without a network. `at` is the offset run.mjs's
# rules pick: for tokenize the last digit that follows a digit, for typed the
# last digit of the first performance's `start`.
tokenize 7 1239: tokenize 17122537640947015359 161 7.000092727189358e+22
typed 7 915: typed 958994639683837840 2
serialize 7 -1: serialize 6303663964400974667 674 9256961296423755170
